            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Russian for a faint, then the Russian for to have the shivers."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: жар",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "жар (zhar) — \"a fever, a high temperature; heat\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "У ребёнка жар, надо вызвать врача. U rebyónka zhar, nádo výzvat vrachá. The child has a fever; we need to call the doctor."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Masculine. У него жар (zhar) — he has a high temperature."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "EtymDB lists жар (zhar) without a source; it is usually traced to Proto-Slavic žarъ 'heat', related to гореть 'to burn' and жарить 'to fry'."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
